,30\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈-1.95,\\,{x}_{2}≈1.19.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-330590" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e05b472e59b38af60f68a33e9dc089a91f10ae2595716a52efa34b80fcb2f832"} {"question" "q-330590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-330590" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1.95"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-330590" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-330590" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.19"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we subtract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e05b472e59b38af60f68a33e9dc089a91f10ae2595716a52efa34b80fcb2f832"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "q-8876" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/state.json" "file_sha256" "075d962bdfee6120ce273fa5867c4a455fd56fc6cb6cbba4393a6e1804358218"} {"question" "e-6192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/example-6192.json" "file_sha256" "7f4c8784936b0144d9082347d17a99e60810e5a81a4f0adb5e9a3bef3ec39689"} {"question" "e-6192" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-7 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions. \n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ.$ To get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{7}{5})+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{54.46}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={125.54}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{125.54}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={305.54}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/example-6192.json" "file_sha256" "7f4c8784936b0144d9082347d17a99e60810e5a81a4f0adb5e9a3bef3ec39689"} {"question" "e-6196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ <  x \\leq 180^\\circ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/example-6196.json" "file_sha256" "e93c255f0cf1f2d2b5ec1271d61a2ca664d97b10cf4457a7dc378423912299a7"} {"question" "e-6196" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{12\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+12 & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-12 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = -75.96^\\circ.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈-{75.96}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={104.04}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are $x= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14055789/example-6196.json" "file_sha256" "e93c255f0cf1f2d2b5ec1271d61a2ca664d97b10cf4457a7dc378423912299a7"}] "usage" {"e-6196" [] "q-8876" [] "e-6192" []} "basis" 1844 "retractions" [[17592186092835 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}\\cos x = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?"] [17592186092033 :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$."] [17592186092033 :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\sin x &= -7\\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n5 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -7 \\\\\n5\\tan x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions.\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\arctan (-\\frac{7}{5}) + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 54.46^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 125.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 125.54^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 305.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ\n$$"] [17592186092034 :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$."] [17592186092034 :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sin x + 12\\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + 12 &= 0 \\\\\n3\\tan x + 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\tan x &= -12 \\\\\n\\tan x &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 75.96^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 104.04^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186091476 17592186091477 17592186091478 17592186091479 17592186091480] "immutable_fields" [[17592186091475 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091475 :answer-field/id] [17592186091475 :answer-field/key] [17592186091475 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
